

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the one remaining architectural unknown before I can present a design: what we build it [3mwith[0m.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this stays a zero-dependency static page or acquires a build step. It affects how the notification
  layer gets written later, how tests run, and how much machinery sits between you and the page.

  - [1mVanilla ES modules, no build[0m — plain [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, split into small files, open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and it runs.
    Nothing to install, nothing to break. Rendering is hand-rolled, which stays fine at this size but gets tedious if
    the app grows a lot.
  - [1mVite + vanilla JS[0m — a dev server, hot reload, and a real test runner (Vitest), still no framework. Adds [38;5;153mnode_modules
[39m    and a build step; buys a much better testing and iteration story, which matters because sub-project 2 will want
    unit tests around event logic.
  - [1mVite + a framework (React/Svelte)[0m — state-driven rendering, which is genuinely nicer once notifications add a badge,
    an inbox, and read state. Biggest dependency footprint, and heavy for a page with a to-do list on it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m Vite + vanilla JS. The no-build option is appealing now and becomes the thing we regret when
  sub-project 2 needs tested event logic and scheduled reminders; a framework is more than this app warrants. Vite is
  the middle that doesn't have to be undone.

  Whichever you pick is also when tooling is cheapest to install, so I've folded that into a second question.
